---
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const manifest = `{
  "endpoints": [
    {
      "id": "user-profile",
      "label": "User profile",
      "method": "GET",
      "path": "/api/users/:id",
      "scenarios": [
        {
          "id": "success",
          "label": "Successful response",
          "status": 200,
          "delay": 250,
          "file": "scenarios/user-success.json"
        },
        {
          "id": "not-found",
          "label": "404 Not Found",
          "status": 404,
          "file": "scenarios/user-not-found.json"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}`;

const rootDelay = `{
  "delay": 400
}`;

const randomDelay = `{
  "delay": [200, 600]
}`;

const directEndpoint = `{
  "endpoints": [
    {
      "id": "json-response",
      "label": "Simple JSON response",
      "method": "GET",
      "path": "/api/demo/json",
      "file": "scenarios/explicit.json",
      "status": 200,
      "delay": 150
    }
  ]
}`;

const directFile = `{
  "endpoints": [
    {
      "path": "/api/demo/json",
      "file": "scenarios/explicit.json"
    }
  ]
}`;

const noContent = `{
  "endpoints": [
    {
      "path": "/api/demo/json",
      "status": 204
    }
  ]
}`;

const schemaManifest = `{
  "$schema": "../node_modules/@wesflo/vite-plugin-local-mock-api/mock.manifest.schema.json",
  "delay": 400
}`;

const diagnosticsConfig = `mockApiPlugin({
  debug: true,
  logging: true
})`;

const multiplePrefixesConfig = `import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import { mockApiPlugin } from '@wesflo/vite-plugin-local-mock-api';

export default defineConfig(({ command }) => ({
  plugins: command === 'serve' ? [
    mockApiPlugin({
      requestPrefixes: ['/api/', '/development-api/'],
    }),
  ] : [],
  server: {
    proxy: {
      '/development-api': {
        target: 'https://api.v1.supertoll.domain.de',
        changeOrigin: true,
        rewrite: (path) => path.replace(/^\\/development-api/, ''),
      },
    },
  },
}));`;
---

<DocsLayout title="Configuration" description="Plugin options, manifests, and file resolution." activePath="/configuration/">
    <article>
        <p class="kicker">Convention before configuration</p>
        <h1>File naming first. Manifest when needed.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
            Most projects only need local files. The plugin derives candidates from the request URL and HTTP method;
            configuration becomes useful when individual calls need exceptional behavior.
        </p>

        <h2 id="file-naming-conventions">File naming conventions</h2>
        <p>
            A URL becomes a path below <code>mockRoot</code>. For each candidate, the plugin checks the uppercased HTTP
            method prefix first and the generic filename second.
        </p>
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Request</th><th>Preferred file</th><th>Generic fallback</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>GET /api/users/profile</code></td><td><code>users/GET_profile.json</code></td><td><code>users/profile.json</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>POST /api/users/profile</code></td><td><code>users/POST_profile.json</code></td><td><code>users/profile.json</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>GET /api/reports/monthly</code></td><td><code>reports/GET_monthly.pdf</code></td><td><code>reports/monthly.pdf</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
            More specific route candidates are tried before suffix and parent fallbacks. First, all candidates for
            <code>users/profile</code> are checked; within each configured extension, the method-prefixed filename wins
            over the generic filename. Only then does resolution continue with the root-level <code>profile</code> suffix.
            Looking only at JSON, <code>PUT /api/users/profile</code> therefore checks
            <code>users/PUT_profile.json</code>, <code>users/profile.json</code>, <code>PUT_profile.json</code>, and
            <code>profile.json</code> in that order. Parent fallbacks follow afterwards.
        </p>
        <p>
            The <code>*</code> candidates use the configured extension order. Supported defaults include JSON, PDF, CSV,
            text, JPEG, PNG, and WebP.
        </p>

        <h2 id="plugin-options">Plugin options</h2>
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>mockRoot</code></td><td><code>URL</code></td><td>Directory containing mock files and the manifest.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>requestPrefixes</code></td><td><code>string | readonly string[]</code></td><td>One or more local request prefixes handled by the plugin. Defaults to <code>['/api/']</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>extensions</code></td><td><code>string[]</code></td><td>Additional file extensions appended to the built-in candidates.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>contentTypes</code></td><td><code>Record&lt;string, string&gt;</code></td><td>Additional or overridden extension-to-content-type mappings.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>manifestFileName</code></td><td><code>string</code></td><td>Manifest filename inside <code>mockRoot</code>. Defaults to <code>mock.manifest.json</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>debug</code></td><td><code>boolean</code></td><td>Enables semantic manifest and referenced-file validation. Defaults to <code>false</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>logging</code></td><td><code>boolean</code></td><td>Logs handled requests and runtime errors. Defaults to <code>true</code>.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
        <Callout title="Fixed route">
            <p><code>MANIFEST_ROUTE</code> is intentionally fixed and cannot be overridden by plugin options.</p>
        </Callout>

        <h3 id="multiple-request-prefixes">Multiple request prefixes and development APIs</h3>
        <p>
            Pass a string for one local URL namespace or an array for several. If prefixes overlap, the longest matching
            value wins regardless of their order. For example, both <code>/api/users</code> and
            <code>/development-api/users</code> resolve against <code>mock/users.*</code> with the configuration below.
        </p>
        <Code code={multiplePrefixesConfig} label="vite.config.ts" />
        <p>
            During local development, point the application at <code>/development-api/</code> instead of making an absolute
            request to the remote domain. Lockxy runs before Vite's proxy: it returns a local response when a mock matches,
            while a request explicitly bypassed through the panel or cookie continues to the configured development API.
            The target may contain any domain or subdomain depth because Vite owns that forwarding step.
        </p>
        <Callout title="Missing mocks stay visible">
            <p>
                A matching request prefix with no local file still produces Lockxy's <code>404</code>. It does not silently
                fall through to the development API. Use an endpoint or global bypass when remote handling is intentional.
                Manifest endpoint paths use the complete local request path, such as
                <code>/development-api/users</code>.
            </p>
        </Callout>

        <h3 id="runtime-file-cache">Runtime file cache</h3>
        <p>
            Lockxy caches successful file-path resolutions in memory by normalized HTTP method and URL pathname, for
            example <code>GET_/api/users</code>. Manifest selection is evaluated first so changing a scenario in the panel
            remains effective. Whenever that selection needs naming-convention fallback, Lockxy checks the same path cache
            before generating and searching all candidates.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>The cache starts empty for every Vite development server.</li>
            <li>Only successful paths are cached; a previous <code>404</code> never hides a file added later.</li>
            <li>Query parameters are excluded because they do not change naming-convention resolution.</li>
            <li>Only the path is cached. File contents are read again, so editing a response remains visible immediately.</li>
            <li>If a cached file is removed, its entry is discarded and the remaining candidates are searched again.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="optional-manifest">Optional manifest</h2>
        <p>
            Create <code>mock.manifest.json</code> only for endpoints that need additional handling. Other requests continue
            to use normal file resolution, so adopting a manifest is not an all-or-nothing decision.
        </p>
        <Callout title="A missing manifest is valid">
            <p>
                The plugin deliberately treats a missing manifest as “no overrides configured,” not as an error. A project
                containing only convention-based mock files therefore remains plug and play. If a manifest exists but is
                malformed, the plugin reports the error instead of treating it as absent.
            </p>
        </Callout>

        <h3 id="json-schema">JSON Schema</h3>
        <p>
            The npm package ships <code>mock.manifest.schema.json</code> for editor autocomplete and inline validation.
            Add <code>$schema</code> to the manifest and adjust the relative path if <code>mockRoot</code> is elsewhere.
            The same schema is also available from the
            <a href="https://wesflo.github.io/vite-plugin-local-mock-api/schema/mock.manifest.schema.json">documentation site</a>.
        </p>
        <Code code={schemaManifest} label="mock/mock.manifest.json" />

        <h3 id="validation-and-logging">Validation and logging</h3>
        <p>
            JSON parsing and the minimum structure required for safe request handling are always checked. Enable
            <code>debug</code> during manifest development for the more expensive checks: invalid status codes and delays,
            unsafe or missing response files, duplicate endpoint or scenario IDs, and active routes that can match the same
            method and path shape. Diagnostics include the exact field path; JSON syntax errors also include line and column.
        </p>
        <Code code={diagnosticsConfig} label="vite.config.ts" />
        <p>
            Request logging is enabled by default and prints method, URL, effective delay, status, and one source word:
            <code>Manifest</code>, <code>Cache</code>, <code>Convention</code>, <code>Passthrough</code>, or
            <code>Error</code>. In an interactive terminal, methods use their familiar request colors, successful and error
            statuses use their status-family colors, delay is orange, and the source is shown in Lockxy's primary color.
            Plain output is used for non-interactive logs and when the <code>NO_COLOR</code> environment variable is set.
            Runtime problems such as corrupt selection cookies, unsafe paths, missing files, and invalid manifests use the
            same <code>[local-mock-api]</code> prefix. Set <code>logging: false</code> for a quiet development server.
        </p>
        <p>
            Every response produced by the mock server includes <code>X-Local-Mock-Api: true</code>, including empty and
            error responses. This makes mocked traffic immediately recognizable in the browser network inspector.
        </p>

        <h3 id="root-fields">Root fields</h3>
        <p>
            The optional root-level <code>delay</code> applies to every mocked API request. This is already a complete
            manifest; an <code>endpoints</code> array is not required:
        </p>
        <Code code={rootDelay} label="mock/mock.manifest.json" />
        <p>
            A two-value range chooses a new inclusive random delay for every request. For example,
            <code>[200, 600]</code> produces an integer delay from 200 through 600 milliseconds. The minimum must not exceed
            the maximum.
        </p>
        <Code code={randomDelay} label="random root delay" />

        <h3 id="direct-endpoint-handling">Direct endpoint handling</h3>
        <p>
            An endpoint can define <code>file</code>, <code>status</code>, and <code>delay</code> directly, independently or
            in combination. Only <code>path</code> is required. Without <code>method</code>, the endpoint matches every HTTP
            method; without <code>file</code>, normal naming-convention resolution remains active.
        </p>
        <Code code={directEndpoint} label="complete endpoint configuration" />
        <Code code={directFile} label="minimal explicit response file" />
        <Code code={noContent} label="file-free 204 response" />
        <p>
            Direct <code>204</code> and <code>304</code> statuses are exceptions to file resolution: they immediately return
            empty responses. Responses to <code>HEAD</code> requests never include a body either.
        </p>

        <h3 id="multiple-scenarios">Multiple scenarios</h3>
        <Code code={manifest} label="mock/mock.manifest.json" />
        <p>
            Exactly one configured scenario is selected automatically—no panel and no scenario cookie are needed. With
            multiple scenarios, the panel or scenario cookie chooses one. Until a choice exists, endpoint-level settings
            and then normal file resolution are used.
        </p>

        <h3 id="endpoint-fields">Endpoint fields</h3>
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Required</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>id</code></td><td>No</td><td>Stable ID for panel controls and cookies. When omitted, it is generated from method and path.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>method</code></td><td>No</td><td>HTTP method matched case-insensitively. When omitted, every method matches.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>path</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Exact path shape. Segments beginning with <code>:</code> match dynamic values.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>label</code></td><td>No</td><td>Human-readable name displayed in the proxy panel.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>active</code></td><td>No</td><td>Set to <code>false</code> to exclude the endpoint from scenario matching.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>file</code></td><td>No</td><td>Explicit response file relative to <code>mockRoot</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>status</code></td><td>No</td><td>HTTP response status. A file-free <code>204</code> returns an empty response.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>delay</code></td><td>No</td><td>Fixed milliseconds or an inclusive <code>[minimum, maximum]</code> range.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>scenarios</code></td><td>No</td><td>One automatic or multiple selectable response variants.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3 id="scenario-fields">Scenario fields</h3>
        <p>
            Scenario <code>id</code> and <code>label</code> are optional. A missing ID is generated from the parent endpoint
            path and the scenario's one-based position; a missing label falls back to the explicit or generated ID. The
            optional <code>file</code> is relative to <code>mockRoot</code>; <code>status</code> changes the HTTP status and
            <code>delay</code> accepts fixed milliseconds or an inclusive random range. If <code>file</code> is omitted,
            normal candidate resolution is used.
            Because generated scenario IDs include their position, define explicit IDs when selections must remain stable
            while scenarios are reordered. If a cookie references a scenario that no longer exists, the first configured
            scenario is used as a safe fallback.
        </p>
        <Callout title="Response precedence">
            <p>
                A selected or automatic scenario overrides endpoint-level <code>file</code>, <code>status</code>, and
                <code>delay</code>. Endpoint values override the root delay. Any remaining file lookup follows the naming
                conventions.
            </p>
        </Callout>
        <Callout title="Typical manifest use cases">
            <p>
                Return a real <code>204</code> response, simulate <code>400</code> or <code>500</code> failures, delay a call
                to test loading and timeout behavior, or point one endpoint at an explicit response file.
            </p>
        </Callout>
    </article>
</DocsLayout>
